    "title": "Visual Canon",
    "category": "Core Canon",
    "content": "# Visual Canon\n\n## Founder population\n\nFounder forms are strongly theropod:\n- feathered\n- toothed\n- long-tailed\n- digitigrade\n- three-fingered\n- enlarged second-toe claw\n- nonhuman posture\n- predatory sensory focus\n\nDo not depict:\n- modern bird beaks\n- human hands\n- human torso proportions\n- generic scaly movie raptors\n- reptilian humanoids\n\n## Deep-time evolution\n\nAfter tens of millions of years, especially after directed biological design begins, descendants can look radically different from founders.\n\nRules:\n- retain terrestrial biological continuity\n- preserve deep anatomical logic rather than superficial founder appearance\n- allow major divergence among lineages\n- allow convergence on efficient upright/manipulative forms\n- do not make them humans with scales\n- do not freeze them as raptors in robes\n\n## Modern New Madrid forms\n\nAt a distance some individuals may read as person-like.\n\nUp close, anatomy should remain clearly non-mammalian and nonhuman.\n\nPossible traits:\n- refined upright posture\n- reduced or vestigial tail in some lineages\n- highly dexterous non-primate hands\n- shortened theropod-derived face\n- altered integument: skin, keratin, filaments, localized feather structures\n- large expressive eyes\n- nonhuman jaw and cranial geometry\n- modified digitigrade or semi-digitigrade gait\n- subtle ancestral scute/keratin patterns\n- sensory crests or filaments\n- extensive lineage-specific variation\n\nHuman observation over millions of years can influence deliberate design, but they remain their own lineage.\n"
